Here is a complete breakdown of AWS Authentication using OIDC (OpenID Connect) 
for Terraform running in GitHub Actions—covering both the theoretical foundation and a step-by-step practical implementation.  

🧠 Part 1: Theory — Why & How OIDC Works

1. The Old Way (Static Access Keys)

Historically, to connect GitHub Actions to AWS, you created an IAM User, generated an AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, and saved them as GitHub Secrets.

  Risks:Keys are long-lived and rarely rotated.  
  If leaked or exposed in logs/repositories, attackers gain persistent access to your AWS account.  
  Requires managing credentials across different tools and team members.  
  
2. The OIDC Way (Keyless & Temporary)

OpenID Connect (OIDC) relies on Federated Identity Management. Instead of storing passwords or static keys, GitHub Actions proves its identity to AWS using a signed JSON Web Token (JWT) issued for every individual job run.

    [ GitHub Actions Runner ] 
            │ 
            │ 1. Request short-lived OIDC Token (JWT)
            ▼
   [ GitHub OIDC Token Issuer ] 
            │ 
            │ 2. Issues signed JWT containing job metadata (repo, branch, event)
            ▼
  [ GitHub Actions Runner ]
            │ 
            │ 3. Calls AWS STS (AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity) presenting JWT
            ▼
     [ AWS STS Service ] 
            │ 
            │ 4. Validates JWT signature with GitHub's OIDC Provider
            │    Checks IAM Role Trust Policy (Repo name, branch match)
            ▼
     [ AWS STS Service ] 
            │ 
            │ 5. Returns temporary, short-lived AWS credentials (valid ~1 hr)
            ▼
  [ GitHub Actions Runner ]
            │ 
            │ 6. Executes `terraform plan / apply` securely!


Key Security Benefits of OIDC

Zero Static Credentials: No AWS Access Keys are saved in GitHub Secrets.  

Short-Lived Credentials: Issued credentials automatically expire after the workflow job completes (default 1 hour).  

Granular Access Control: You can write trust policies restricting AWS access to a specific repository, environment, or branch (e.g., allow terraform apply only from the master branch of your specific repository).  

🛠️ Part 2: Practical Setup — Step-by-Step

We will configure this using AWS IAM and GitHub Actions.  

Step 1: Create the OIDC Identity Provider in AWS IAM

Open AWS Management Console  IAM  Identity Providers. 

Click Add provider. 

Select OpenID Connect.  

Fill in details:

Provider URL: [https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com](https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com)

Click Get thumbprint  

Audience: sts.amazonaws.com  

Click Add provider.  

(Save the Provider ARN, which will look like: arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com)  

Step 2: Create the IAM Role & Trust Policy

Go to IAM Roles Create role.

Select Custom trust policy and paste the following policy:

⚠️ Replace <ACCOUNT_ID>, <YOUR_GITHUB_ORGANIZATION_OR_USERNAME>, and <YOUR_REPO_NAME> with your actual values.  

JSON{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud": "sts.amazonaws.com"
        },
        "StringLike": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:<YOUR_GITHUB_ORGANIZATION_OR_USERNAME>/<YOUR_REPO_NAME>:*"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

 Attach permissions to this role required by Terraform (e.g., AdministratorAccess or custom policies for S3, EC2, VPC, DynamoDB).

 Name the role: GitHubActions-Terraform-Role and save it.

Step 3: Update your GitHub Actions Workflow (terraform.yml)

In your GitHub repository, open .github/workflows/terraform.yml.  

Two key elements are needed:

permissions: id-token: write — Gives GitHub Actions permission to generate the JWT token.

aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials configured with role-to-assume (without specifying access keys).  

YAML

name: "Terraform CI/CD with OIDC"

on:
  push:
    branches: [ "master" ]
  pull_request:
    branches: [ "master" ]

# 🔑 CRITICAL: Required for GitHub Actions to request the OIDC JWT token
permissions:
  id-token: write # Required for requesting the JWT
  contents: read  # Required for checkout

jobs:
  terraform:
    name: "Terraform Execution"
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      # 1. Checkout repository
      - name: Checkout Code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      # 2. Setup Terraform
      - name: Setup Terraform
        uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3
        with:
          terraform_version: "1.15.0"

      # 3. Authenticate with AWS using OIDC Role Assumption (No Access Keys!)
      - name: Configure AWS Credentials via OIDC
        uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
        with:
          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/GitHubActions-Terraform-Role
          aws-region: us-west-2

      # 4. Terraform Init
      - name: Terraform Init
        run: terraform init

      # 5. Terraform Format Check
      - name: Terraform Format Check
        run: terraform fmt -check

      # 6. Terraform Plan
      - name: Terraform Plan
        run: terraform plan -no-color -input=false

      # 7. Terraform Apply (runs only on push to master)
      - name: Terraform Apply
        if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/master' && github.event_name == 'push'
        run: terraform apply -auto-approve -input=false


Step 4: Clean Up Old GitHub Secrets

Once OIDC is active, you can completely remove AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY from your repository's Settings  Secrets and variables  Actions.  

Your GitHub Actions runner will assume the IAM role dynamically during execution and discard credentials as soon as the run finishes.  

##----
Here is the complete Terraform code to automate creating the IAM OIDC Identity Provider, 
IAM Role, and Trust Policy.

You can add this code directly into your repository 
(for example, in a new file named oidc.tf or inside your main.tf).

oidc.tf

Terraform
################################################################################
# GitHub Data Source & OIDC Provider
################################################################################

# GitHub's OIDC Provider TLS Certificate Thumbprint
# Standard root thumbprint for token.actions.githubusercontent.com
data "tls_certificate" "github" {
  url = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com/.well-known/openid-configuration"
}

# Create the OIDC Provider in AWS IAM
resource "aws_iam_openid_connect_provider" "github" {
  url             = "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
  client_id_list  = ["sts.amazonaws.com"]
  thumbprint_list = [data.tls_certificate.github.certificates[0].sha1_fingerprint]

  tags = {
    Environment = "production"
    ManagedBy   = "Terraform"
  }
}

################################################################################
# IAM Role & Trust Policy for GitHub Actions
################################################################################

# Local variables to configure repository constraints easily
locals {
  github_username_or_org = "YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME" # e.g., "abdul-repo"
  github_repo_name       = "YOUR_REPO_NAME"        # e.g., "learn-terraform-get-started-aws"
}

# Create the IAM Role that GitHub Actions will assume
resource "aws_iam_role" "github_actions" {
  name = "GitHubActions-Terraform-Role"

  # Trust policy restricting access strictly to your repository
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Federated = aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.github.arn
        }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"
        Condition = {
          StringEquals = {
            "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud" = "sts.amazonaws.com"
          }
          StringLike = {
            # Limits role assumption to your specific GitHub repo across all branches and events
            "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub" = "repo:${local.github_username_or_org}/${local.github_repo_name}:*"
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  })

  tags = {
    Environment = "production"
    ManagedBy   = "Terraform"
  }
}

################################################################################
# Attach Permissions to the Role
################################################################################

# Attach AdministratorAccess (or custom policies) to allow Terraform to manage resources
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "github_actions_admin" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.github_actions.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess"
}

################################################################################
# Output the Role ARN
################################################################################

output "github_actions_role_arn" {
  description = "ARN of the IAM Role for GitHub Actions OIDC"
  value       = aws_iam_role.github_actions.arn
}

How to use this:
Copy the code into a file named oidc.tf in your root directory.

Update local.github_username_or_org and local.github_repo_name at lines 28–29 with your actual GitHub username/organization and repository name.

Run terraform apply locally once (using your local AWS credentials).

Terraform will output the github_actions_role_arn. Copy this ARN and paste it into your .github/workflows/terraform.yml pipeline file under role-to-assume.